1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:00,900
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,422 --> 00:00:04,452
Today is Agent Design Patterns and Scaffolds.

3
00:00:04,878 --> 00:00:14,372
A scaffold, sometimes called a harness, is all the code around the model, the loop, the tools, and the logic that decides what goes into the prompt.

4
00:00:14,862 --> 00:00:16,548
Last week we looked at the parts.

5
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:22,660
Today is about how people put those parts together and which arrangements tend to work.

6
00:00:23,694 --> 00:00:25,060
A few logistics first.

7
00:00:25,742 --> 00:00:27,760
Get your Google credit request in today.

8
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:31,680
If you haven't, follow the ICLR format for your reports.

9
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:38,532
Homework 1 is due Friday, October 30th, and the midpoint demo is November 4th with the report on the 6th.

10
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,220
So, design patterns.

11
00:00:42,190 --> 00:00:43,820
Some of you will know this book.

12
00:00:44,494 --> 00:00:51,492
It's the Gang of 4 book from 1994, and it gave programmers shared names for recurring solutions.

13
00:00:52,014 --> 00:00:59,200
On the right is one of them, the singleton, which is a class that only ever allows one copy of itself to exist.

14
00:00:59,662 --> 00:01:02,596
The point of a name like that isn't the code.

15
00:01:03,118 --> 00:01:09,956
It's that when someone says make it a singleton, everyone in the room knows the shape of the solution and its trade-offs.

16
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,548
That's what we want for agents today.

17
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:21,220
A shared set of names, so you can look at a system and say, oh, that's an orchestrator with workers, plus an evaluator loop.

18
00:01:22,318 --> 00:01:28,484
And here's the honest caveat from Martin Bowler, who wrote a well-known book on enterprise software patterns.

19
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,284
Patents are half-baked, you always have to finish them yourself.

20
00:01:34,030 --> 00:01:37,636
So don't treat anything today as a recipe, you copy exactly.

21
00:01:38,702 --> 00:01:43,524
When you build an agent, you make lots of small design decisions, often without noticing.

22
00:01:43,918 --> 00:01:45,988
Today we'll name the common ones.

23
00:01:46,766 --> 00:01:49,840
In practice, you will adjust each one to fit your problem.

24
00:01:50,894 --> 00:02:04,548
By the end, you should be able to name the common patterns for workflows and agents, compare the different ways agents manage their context, and look at a real production harness, one called Pi, and point out the design decisions it makes.

25
00:02:05,678 --> 00:02:06,660
Here's the plan.

26
00:02:07,278 --> 00:02:21,284
A quick review of workflows versus agents, then the workflow patterns, then patterns for how an agent reasons and acts, then patterns for managing context, which is where a lot of the real engineering happens.

27
00:02:21,902 --> 00:02:23,908
And we'll finish with the case study.

28
00:02:25,134 --> 00:02:26,820
Let's start with a review.

29
00:02:27,982 --> 00:02:30,080
Here's the question that sorts everything.

30
00:02:30,382 --> 00:02:32,160
Where does the control flow live?

31
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:35,844
Control flow just means who decides what happens next.

32
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:38,160
On the left is a bare model.

33
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:41,520
It answers one prompt, and that's it.

34
00:02:41,902 --> 00:02:45,188
It's a component, a small part of some larger system.

35
00:02:45,614 --> 00:02:50,020
In the middle is a workflow which people also call a compound AI system.

36
00:02:50,414 --> 00:02:54,400
The model gets called several times, but your program decides the order.

37
00:02:54,734 --> 00:02:57,796
An email categorizer is a good example.

38
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:01,920
Read the email, call the model to label it, file it.

39
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:06,564
At the right end is an agent, where the model itself decides the next step.

40
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:10,180
A customer service agent or a coding agent works this way.

41
00:03:10,542 --> 00:03:13,200
Let me introduce today's running example.

42
00:03:13,614 --> 00:03:18,720
Say the City Transit Office asks you to build an assistant that writes a one-page briefing.

43
00:03:18,926 --> 00:03:21,796
Should our city switch its bus fleet to electric?

44
00:03:22,158 --> 00:03:25,956
Keep that in mind because we'll build it several different ways today.

45
00:03:27,022 --> 00:03:30,596
This picture from a Lenching blog post shows the trade-off.

46
00:03:31,022 --> 00:03:37,156
As you give the system more agency, meaning more freedom to choose its own steps, it becomes less predictable.

47
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:40,996
Workflows are predictable because the steps are written in code.

48
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:43,876
You can read the code and know what will happen.

49
00:03:44,238 --> 00:03:50,436
Agents are flexible because the model chooses the steps so they can handle situations you didn't plan for.

50
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:55,204
But the same freedom means two runs on the same input can go very differently.

51
00:03:55,694 --> 00:03:58,052
Notice the curve isn't a straight line.

52
00:03:58,478 --> 00:04:03,044
A little agency costs you little predictability, but past some point it drops fast.

53
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:07,940
So the design question is never a workflow or agent in the abstract.

54
00:04:08,622 --> 00:04:11,780
It's how much freedom does this particular step need?

55
00:04:12,846 --> 00:04:15,332
Here's what that looks like as actual graphs.

56
00:04:15,822 --> 00:04:17,408
On the left is a workflow.

57
00:04:17,648 --> 00:04:22,768
Extract the event from a message, pick an emoji, add it to the calendar.

58
00:04:23,008 --> 00:04:23,396
Done.

59
00:04:23,822 --> 00:04:25,924
Every path is drawn in advance.

60
00:04:26,638 --> 00:04:29,008
On the right is an agent.

61
00:04:29,582 --> 00:04:33,328
There's a model, there are tools, and there's a loop between them.

62
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:37,092
The model keeps choosing tools until it decides to stop.

63
00:04:37,614 --> 00:04:42,788
The whole agent is that one small loop, and every decision happens inside it.

64
00:04:43,758 --> 00:04:47,248
This table sums it up, and I just want to point at the bottom line.

65
00:04:47,662 --> 00:04:50,596
Real systems often combine workflows and agents.

66
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:53,860
Take the support ticket example on the slide.

67
00:04:54,254 --> 00:04:58,788
The workflow version is classify the ticket, look up the policy, and draft a reply.

68
00:04:59,214 --> 00:05:04,516
The agent version is go investigate and fix the problem using whatever tools you need.

69
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:14,020
For our bus briefing, a sensible design might be a workflow on the outside, with fixed sections the city always wants, like cost, emissions, and risks.

70
00:05:14,478 --> 00:05:17,924
Then inside one section say, What will charging cost?

71
00:05:18,128 --> 00:05:19,648
You let an agent go dig.

72
00:05:20,206 --> 00:05:24,260
You get predictability where you need it and flexibility where you need it.

73
00:05:34,734 --> 00:05:40,932
It defines workflows as systems where the model and tools are put together through predefined code paths.

74
00:05:41,518 --> 00:05:44,772
Agents are systems where the model directs its own process.

75
00:05:45,326 --> 00:05:48,356
The same post gives advice I tape to your monitor.

76
00:05:48,942 --> 00:05:55,204
Start with the simplest thing that works and call the model API directly before reaching for a framework.

77
00:05:55,598 --> 00:05:58,468
Many of these patterns are a few lines of code.

78
00:05:58,926 --> 00:06:02,628
Only add steps when you can show the simpler version falls short.

79
00:06:03,086 --> 00:06:09,252
Frameworks are handy, but they can hide the actual prompts and responses, and that makes debugging harder.

80
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:18,500
There are five patterns Prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator workers, and evaluator optimizer.

81
00:06:18,830 --> 00:06:23,088
Let's take them one at a time quickly, because each one is simple.

82
00:06:23,438 --> 00:06:25,668
The interesting part is combining them.

83
00:06:26,798 --> 00:06:27,876
Prompt chaining.

84
00:06:28,366 --> 00:06:33,572
You break a task into steps and each model call works on the output of the previous one.

85
00:06:34,158 --> 00:06:36,964
The purple box in the middle is called a gate.

86
00:06:37,358 --> 00:06:39,556
It's a plain code check between steps.

87
00:06:39,982 --> 00:06:45,892
If step one's output fails the check, you stop early instead of passing garbage down the line.

88
00:06:46,318 --> 00:06:48,768
For our briefing, step one writes an outline.

89
00:06:48,928 --> 00:06:52,516
A gate checks that the outline has all the sections the city requires.

90
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:56,420
Step 2 drafts each section, and step 3 edits for length.

91
00:06:56,910 --> 00:06:58,928
The trade-off is on the slide.

92
00:06:59,246 --> 00:07:06,436
You pay in latency, because there are more calls, and you get accuracy, because each call has an easier job.

93
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:08,260
Routing.

94
00:07:08,814 --> 00:07:12,772
First you classify the input, and then you send it to the right handler.

95
00:07:13,168 --> 00:07:16,164
That could be a different prompt or a different model.

96
00:07:16,590 --> 00:07:18,340
There are two reasons to do this.

97
00:07:19,182 --> 00:07:20,448
One is quality.

98
00:07:20,878 --> 00:07:30,148
A question about bus battery chemistry and a question about union contracts need different instructions, and one prompt that tries to cover both does both badly.

99
00:07:30,542 --> 00:07:32,004
The other reason is money.

100
00:07:32,494 --> 00:07:37,540
Send the easy questions to a small cheap model and save the expensive model for the hard ones.

101
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:41,568
The catch is that the whole thing is only as good as the classifier.

102
00:07:41,902 --> 00:07:47,332
If it sends a hard question to the cheap model, nothing later in the pipeline catches that.

103
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:49,540
Parallelization.

104
00:07:50,030 --> 00:07:53,444
Run several model calls at the same time and combine the results.

105
00:07:53,998 --> 00:07:55,332
There are two versions.

106
00:07:56,110 --> 00:07:58,564
Sectioning means splitting into independent pieces.

107
00:07:59,022 --> 00:08:01,060
The example on the slide is a good one.

108
00:08:01,742 --> 00:08:07,808
While one call answers the user's question, a separate call checks whether the question is safe to answer.

109
00:08:08,270 --> 00:08:15,968
Anthropics post notes This tends to work better than asking one call to do both, because each call can focus on one thing.

110
00:08:16,334 --> 00:08:20,644
Voting means running the same task several times and combining the answers.

111
00:08:21,166 --> 00:08:28,772
That's a form of what people call inference timescaling, where you spend more compute when the model runs instead of training a bigger model.

112
00:08:29,198 --> 00:08:36,388
For the briefing, you could ask three separate calls whether a cost estimate looks plausible and only flag it if two of them say no.

113
00:08:37,646 --> 00:08:38,948
Orchestrator workers.

114
00:08:39,598 --> 00:08:47,076
One model, the orchestrator, looks at the task, decides what subtasks are needed, and hands each one to a worker.

115
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:49,060
Then it combines the results.

116
00:08:49,646 --> 00:08:52,272
How is this different from parallelization?

117
00:08:52,622 --> 00:08:56,324
In parallelization, you wrote the split in code ahead of time.

118
00:08:56,750 --> 00:09:02,020
Here the orchestrator decides the split at runtime, because you can't know it in advance.

119
00:09:02,702 --> 00:09:15,492
If the city asks, compare electric buses to what we have now, the orchestrator might decide it needs one worker per bus maker, plus one for the electricity utility, plus one for the maintenance budget.

120
00:09:15,886 --> 00:09:18,916
A different question would get a different set of workers.

121
00:09:19,406 --> 00:09:22,052
Anthropics Research feature works this way.

122
00:09:22,542 --> 00:09:27,460
A lead agent plans the research and starts several sub-agents that search at the same time.

123
00:09:27,822 --> 00:09:33,412
In the write-up, this setup beat a single agent by about 90% on their internal research tests.

124
00:09:33,966 --> 00:09:35,204
But look at the price.

125
00:09:36,014 --> 00:09:42,884
They report that agents use about four times the tokens of a normal chat, and multi-agent systems about 15 times.

126
00:09:43,502 --> 00:09:48,192
So orchestrator workers is worth it when the task is valuable and splits keenly.

127
00:09:48,686 --> 00:09:51,108
For a two-line answer, it's a waste.

128
00:09:52,206 --> 00:09:54,340
Evaluator Optimizer.

129
00:09:54,670 --> 00:09:59,556
One call writes, another call critiques, and you loop until the critic is satisfied.

130
00:10:00,046 --> 00:10:03,428
The slide lists the conditions and they're worth taking seriously.

131
00:10:03,886 --> 00:10:11,012
You need feedback that can actually be put into words, like the cost section never mentions charging infrastructure.

132
00:10:11,534 --> 00:10:18,404
You need a model that can reliably give that feedback, and the writer has to be able to improve when it gets it.

133
00:10:18,734 --> 00:10:22,116
If any of those is missing, the loop just goes around in circles.

134
00:10:22,574 --> 00:10:25,632
Hold a reporting once about twice minutes.

135
00:10:25,806 --> 00:10:29,796
We'll see a paper showing what happens when the critic has no real information.

136
00:10:30,286 --> 00:10:35,108
For our briefing, the aviator could be a model given the city's checklist.

137
00:10:35,438 --> 00:10:38,244
Does every cost number have a source and a year?

138
00:10:38,542 --> 00:10:41,232
Does the summary actually answer yes or no?

139
00:10:41,518 --> 00:10:42,948
Is it under one page?

140
00:10:43,342 --> 00:10:46,724
Those are things a model can check and a writer can fix.

141
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:50,052
Compare that with make it more persuasive.

142
00:10:50,574 --> 00:10:51,312
That's vague.

143
00:10:51,472 --> 00:10:56,196
The critic's opinion will change from run to run, and the loop never settles.

144
00:10:57,326 --> 00:10:59,748
Now, real systems are rarely one pattern.

145
00:11:00,206 --> 00:11:02,244
They're combinations of these patterns.

146
00:11:03,502 --> 00:11:09,072
Here's a real one storm from a Stanford group published at NA Sekle 2024.

147
00:11:09,454 --> 00:11:19,152
You give it a topic, here the 2024 Hualian City earthquake in Taiwan, and it writes a full Wikipedia-style article with a table of contents and citations.

148
00:11:19,694 --> 00:11:22,660
On the right, you can see the output several pages long.

149
00:11:23,022 --> 00:11:26,148
That's very close to our briefing problem, just longer.

150
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:28,740
Here's how it works.

151
00:11:29,262 --> 00:11:36,356
The name stands for Synthesis of Topic Outlines through retrieval and multi-perspective question asking.

152
00:11:36,752 --> 00:11:38,020
Follow the numbers.

153
00:11:38,574 --> 00:11:42,692
First, it surveys related articles to see what topics like this usually cover.

154
00:11:43,054 --> 00:11:47,872
Then it comes up with several perspectives, different kinds of people who'd care about this topic.

155
00:11:48,238 --> 00:11:52,836
For the earthquake, maybe a seismologist, an emergency planter, and a resident.

156
00:11:53,166 --> 00:11:56,484
Then for each perspective, it simulates a conversation.

157
00:11:56,878 --> 00:12:04,420
A writer bot with that perspective asks questions, and an expert bot answers them by searching the web and reading trusted sources.

158
00:12:04,814 --> 00:12:08,836
Finally, it gathers all those conversations and uses them to refine an outline.

159
00:12:09,422 --> 00:12:10,532
Why the role play?

160
00:12:11,182 --> 00:12:17,412
Because if you just ask a model what should an article about this cover, you get the obvious topics.

161
00:12:17,934 --> 00:12:22,788
Asking questions from different viewpoints turns up the things a single viewpoint would miss.

162
00:12:24,046 --> 00:12:27,204
And here's the same diagram with our patterns drawn on top.

163
00:12:27,598 --> 00:12:30,628
The survey and draft outline steps are a prompt chain.

164
00:12:31,086 --> 00:12:36,132
The conversations from different perspectives run side by side, which is parallelization.

165
00:12:36,526 --> 00:12:49,796
Generating perspectives and handing each one to a conversation is roughly orchestrator workers, though as the footnote says, here it's close to plain parallelization because the number of perspectives is fixed.

166
00:12:50,254 --> 00:12:55,588
And the back and forth between writer and expert is roughly an evaluator-optimizer loop.

167
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:00,324
So a system that looks complicated is really four simple patterns put together.

168
00:13:00,750 --> 00:13:02,640
That's the skill I want you to build.

169
00:13:02,926 --> 00:13:05,476
Look at a diagram like this and see the pieces.

170
00:13:06,702 --> 00:13:07,556
How does it work?

171
00:13:08,078 --> 00:13:10,880
Don't worry about every number in the table.

172
00:13:11,246 --> 00:13:17,028
The comparison that matters is the bottom two rows, Storm, and Storm without the outline stage.

173
00:13:17,390 --> 00:13:22,340
Take out the outline step and the scores dropped back toward the plain baselines.

174
00:13:22,958 --> 00:13:25,828
So the planning step is doing a lot of the work.

175
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:28,772
The paper also had human evaluation.

176
00:13:29,230 --> 00:13:37,028
Experienced Wikipedia editors rated Storm's articles as better organized and broader in coverage than a retrieval baseline.

177
00:13:37,454 --> 00:13:44,228
They also pointed out problems like the system picking up bias from its sources or connecting facts that weren't really related.

178
00:13:45,230 --> 00:13:46,788
Here's a different question.

179
00:13:47,342 --> 00:13:51,620
If systems are combinations of patents, which combination should you use?

180
00:13:52,174 --> 00:13:56,100
Arcon from a team at Stanford turns that into a search problem.

181
00:13:56,430 --> 00:14:07,140
Look at the inputs on the left, a benchmark you care about, a budget for how many model calls you can afford, a set of available models, and a menu of techniques.

182
00:14:07,566 --> 00:14:15,760
The optimizer in the middle tries a combination, tests it on sample problems, and uses the result to pick the next combination to try.

183
00:14:16,078 --> 00:14:24,900
The method is called Bayesian optimization, which just means a search that uses past results to guess which untried option is most promising.

184
00:14:26,126 --> 00:14:28,068
Here's the menu of building blocks.

185
00:14:28,686 --> 00:14:31,440
Generator writes a candidate answer.

186
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:34,660
Fuser merges several candidates into one.

187
00:14:35,182 --> 00:14:37,520
Critic lists strengths and weaknesses.

188
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:39,840
Ranker picks the best few.

189
00:14:40,622 --> 00:14:46,320
Verifier checks the reasoning, plus a pair that writes unit tests and runs them.

190
00:14:46,734 --> 00:14:48,036
Look at the cost column.

191
00:14:48,462 --> 00:14:51,200
Every one of these is one or two model calls.

192
00:14:51,694 --> 00:14:54,980
They're simple pieces and you get the power from stacking them.

193
00:14:56,142 --> 00:14:58,820
Here's what the search found for math problems.

194
00:14:59,278 --> 00:15:04,004
Four different generators, each from a different model, each sampled five times.

195
00:15:04,494 --> 00:15:06,372
Then a critic and a ranker.

196
00:15:06,862 --> 00:15:08,452
Then four fusers.

197
00:15:09,198 --> 00:15:11,652
Then a verifier and a final fuser.

198
00:15:12,110 --> 00:15:14,640
No person would have designed exactly this.

199
00:15:15,022 --> 00:15:23,844
It's worth noticing that it mixes models from several companies because different models make different mistakes, and a fuser can combine their strengths.

200
00:15:24,974 --> 00:15:26,240
And here's the result.

201
00:15:26,734 --> 00:15:32,000
The x-axis is how many tokens you're allowed to spend, and a blue line is ARCON.

202
00:15:32,366 --> 00:15:40,356
As the budget grows, ARCON keeps improving and passes the purple dotted line, which is OpenAI's O1, a reasoning model.

203
00:15:40,814 --> 00:15:51,204
The paper reports about a 15% average improvement over 01, GPT-4.0, and Clot 3.5 Sonnet across its benchmarks when given extra budget.

204
00:15:51,630 --> 00:15:54,180
The catch is in the X-axis label.

205
00:15:54,510 --> 00:15:57,444
This costs a lot of tokens per question.

206
00:15:57,838 --> 00:16:04,836
Combining many calls this way makes sense when correctness matters more than cost, and it's overkill for a chatbot.

207
00:16:05,326 --> 00:16:08,068
The other lines on the chat are worth a sentence.

208
00:16:08,718 --> 00:16:22,596
ADAS and a flow are two other research systems that also search automatically for good agent designs, and MOA, short for mixture of agents, is a fixed recipe where several models answer and later layers combine their answers.

209
00:16:23,150 --> 00:16:26,852
So automatic search over designs is now its own research area.

210
00:16:27,246 --> 00:16:34,756
You saw a close cousin of this idea last week with ASP, which automatically tunes the prompts inside a pipeline.

211
00:16:35,918 --> 00:16:40,868
To recap workflows, they're easier to reproduce and debug than agents.

212
00:16:41,390 --> 00:16:47,236
There are five basic patterns, and real systems are usually larger combinations of them.

213
00:16:48,334 --> 00:16:52,228
Now let's move to agents, starting with how they reason and act.

214
00:16:53,326 --> 00:16:55,040
Here's the agents loop again.

215
00:16:55,200 --> 00:17:00,196
The model calls an action on the environment, gets feedback, and repeats until it stops.

216
00:17:00,750 --> 00:17:03,268
The reg text makes the point for this section.

217
00:17:03,662 --> 00:17:06,180
Giving the agent tools isn't enough.

218
00:17:06,734 --> 00:17:10,720
You also have to design how it reasons and how it interacts with the world.

219
00:17:11,086 --> 00:17:14,372
Each paper in this section changes one piece of that loop.

220
00:17:15,456 --> 00:17:18,148
First up is React, the reading for today.

221
00:17:18,766 --> 00:17:21,924
Its key idea is to reason before acting.

222
00:17:22,976 --> 00:17:24,548
You've seen this slide before.

223
00:17:25,198 --> 00:17:28,132
It's the Apple Remote Example from Lecture 1.

224
00:17:28,576 --> 00:17:29,572
Quick Reminder.

225
00:17:30,062 --> 00:17:35,140
Reasoning alone, the chain of thought box confidently builds on a false first step.

226
00:17:35,470 --> 00:17:42,576
Acting alone, the middle box searches but never stops to think about what it found, so it gives a nonsense answer.

227
00:17:42,926 --> 00:17:44,804
React does both in turn.

228
00:17:45,006 --> 00:17:48,068
Think, search, read the result, think again.

229
00:17:48,398 --> 00:17:52,484
And when a search fails, it reads the error and searches more precisely.

230
00:17:53,102 --> 00:17:57,444
Remember also from Lecture 1 that React swaps one kind of failure for another.

231
00:17:57,806 --> 00:18:03,140
It rarely makes things up, but it can get stuck in loops or be thrown off by bad search results.

232
00:18:03,662 --> 00:18:09,284
Every pattern for the rest of this section is, in part, a fix for one of those failures.

233
00:18:10,446 --> 00:18:13,572
Here's a part of the paper people often skip.

234
00:18:13,998 --> 00:18:17,636
Look at the left chart where they just prompt the model with examples.

235
00:18:18,096 --> 00:18:26,180
React, the red bar, is actually worse for the small 8 billion parameter model, and it isn't the best, even at 540 billion.

236
00:18:26,574 --> 00:18:34,532
Now look at the right chart where they fine-tune the models, meaning they train them a bit more on about 3000 example React trajectories.

237
00:18:34,894 --> 00:18:43,172
Now React is the best method at both sizes, and the fine-tuned 62 billion model beats the best prompted 540 billion one.

238
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:50,276
So even in 2022, the lesson was that acting like an agent is a skill models get better at with training.

239
00:18:50,576 --> 00:18:52,164
Today that's standard.

240
00:18:52,622 --> 00:19:00,868
Model companies train in models on agent-style tool use as part of post-training, which is the training done after the base model is built.

241
00:19:01,198 --> 00:19:07,204
It's a big part of why current models are so much better at agent tasks than the models in these papers.

242
00:19:08,462 --> 00:19:11,108
And here's the comparison that shows thinking matters.

243
00:19:11,630 --> 00:19:15,204
Same model, same tools, with and without the bot steps.

244
00:19:15,758 --> 00:19:19,536
React with thoughts beats, act without thoughts, on both benchmarks.

245
00:19:19,886 --> 00:19:23,172
The gaps are small, a couple of points, but it holds up.

246
00:19:23,502 --> 00:19:29,056
And this think, then call a tool pattern is still how nearly every agent works today.

247
00:19:29,486 --> 00:19:34,052
Why do the thoughts help if the model could just call the tool directly?

248
00:19:34,478 --> 00:19:38,340
The paper's answer is that thoughts let the agent keep track of the plan.

249
00:19:38,734 --> 00:19:46,404
It can write down what it's looking for, notice when a result doesn't fit, and decide what to try next when something goes wrong.

250
00:19:46,798 --> 00:19:52,096
Without the thought, every action has to carry all of that in the model's head, invisibly.

251
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:56,036
With it, the plan is right there in the context for the next step to read.

252
00:19:57,134 --> 00:19:58,116
Next pattern.

253
00:19:58,574 --> 00:20:01,284
React gave us think, then act.

254
00:20:01,900 --> 00:20:03,684
Reflection adds one more step.

255
00:20:03,886 --> 00:20:07,524
When you fail, stop and write down why and then try again.

256
00:20:07,982 --> 00:20:09,988
Let's bring back our running example.

257
00:20:10,446 --> 00:20:16,676
Our research assistant is writing a one-page briefing on whether the city should switch its buses to electric.

258
00:20:17,166 --> 00:20:25,092
Say its first draft comes back from the city's fact checker marked wrong because it quoted the charging cost for the wrong year.

259
00:20:25,454 --> 00:20:28,460
A person in that spot doesn't just start over and hope.

260
00:20:28,846 --> 00:20:33,380
They think, I grabbed the first number I found, and it was from an old report.

261
00:20:33,870 --> 00:20:38,180
Next time I check the date, that sentence is the whole idea of reflection.

262
00:20:39,214 --> 00:20:40,260
Here's how it's built.

263
00:20:40,686 --> 00:20:42,884
Look at the box diagram on the left.

264
00:20:43,502 --> 00:20:45,924
There are three language model rows.

265
00:20:46,574 --> 00:20:51,216
The actor is the one doing the task, the normal React agent.

266
00:20:51,630 --> 00:21:02,404
The evaluator looks at what the actor did and gives a score, either from a rule, from another model, or straight from the environment, like a test passing or failing.

267
00:21:02,798 --> 00:21:10,884
And the self-reflection model takes that score and writes a few sentences of plain text about what went wrong and what to do differently.

268
00:21:11,406 --> 00:21:14,852
Now the key part, and it's the line at the bottom of the slide.

269
00:21:15,374 --> 00:21:19,940
Those reflection sentences get saved and pasted into the actor's prompt on the next try.

270
00:21:20,430 --> 00:21:24,164
The paper calls this long-term memory, but there's nothing fancy here.

271
00:21:24,494 --> 00:21:26,532
It's text in the context window.

272
00:21:27,214 --> 00:21:29,252
Nobody retrains the model.

273
00:21:29,774 --> 00:21:31,620
Its weights never change.

274
00:21:32,110 --> 00:21:36,868
The authors describe this as reinforcement learning with words instead of wait update.

275
00:21:37,230 --> 00:21:42,436
In normal reinforcement learning, a failure turns into a number that nudges the model's weights.

276
00:21:43,022 --> 00:21:46,372
Here, a failure turns into a sentence the model can read.

277
00:21:46,766 --> 00:21:47,524
That's cheap.

278
00:21:47,758 --> 00:21:55,044
It works with any model you can call through an API, and you can read the reflections yourself to see what the agent thinks it did wrong.

279
00:21:55,406 --> 00:21:59,012
The catch is that this only works when you get more than one try.

280
00:21:59,310 --> 00:22:07,108
Reflection is a loop over attempts, so it fits tasks you can rerun, like called against tests or a quiz with an answer key.

281
00:22:07,502 --> 00:22:11,588
If the action is send the briefing to the city council, you don't get a second attempt.

282
00:22:12,718 --> 00:22:15,748
Here are three examples from the paper, and the colors help.

283
00:22:16,206 --> 00:22:18,852
Red is where things went wrong, green is the fix.

284
00:22:19,374 --> 00:22:23,140
On the left, the agent is in a simulated house trying to clean a pan.

285
00:22:23,502 --> 00:22:29,028
It tried to pick up the pan from the wrong burner, and the environment said nothing happens.

286
00:22:29,646 --> 00:22:36,304
The reflection says basically the pan wasn't on stove burner one, and on the next try it goes to burner two.

287
00:22:36,782 --> 00:22:38,884
In the middle, it's writing code.

288
00:22:39,310 --> 00:22:41,220
It writes its own unit tests.

289
00:22:41,646 --> 00:22:47,876
A test fails, and the reflection says, I only check the total count of parentheses, not their order.

290
00:22:48,526 --> 00:22:51,424
That's a real bug diagnosis in one sentence.

291
00:22:51,854 --> 00:22:54,180
On the right is a trivia question.

292
00:22:54,734 --> 00:22:57,764
The answer was wrong and the only feedback was a zero.

293
00:22:58,094 --> 00:23:00,676
Notice how little the agent was told there.

294
00:23:01,104 --> 00:23:02,116
Just wrong.

295
00:23:02,702 --> 00:23:09,636
From that it worked out on its own that it had assumed both people shared two professions when they only shared one.

296
00:23:10,030 --> 00:23:12,868
Ask yourself what all three have in common.

297
00:23:13,262 --> 00:23:17,092
In every case, something outside the model said that didn't work.

298
00:23:17,454 --> 00:23:19,780
The hows, the test, the answer key.

299
00:23:20,174 --> 00:23:21,156
Keep that in mind.

300
00:23:21,486 --> 00:23:23,428
Because it's about to matter a lot.

301
00:23:24,590 --> 00:23:25,444
Does it help?

302
00:23:25,902 --> 00:23:27,396
Look at the left chart.

303
00:23:27,918 --> 00:23:32,036
The flat gray lines are chain of thought and react on their own.

304
00:23:32,430 --> 00:23:39,076
You can rerun them as many times as you like, and they don't get better, because nothing carries over between tries.

305
00:23:39,598 --> 00:23:47,844
The blue line is React plus Reflection, and it climbs with each trial, from about a third of questions solved to more than half.

306
00:23:48,366 --> 00:23:58,624
On a coding benchmark called Humanevil, the code reflection finally submitted passed on 91% of problems compared with 80% for GPT-4 on its own.

307
00:23:59,054 --> 00:24:04,100
The right chart is the experiment I really want you to look at, because it answers a fair objection.

308
00:24:04,526 --> 00:24:06,916
Maybe reflection isn't doing anything.

309
00:24:07,310 --> 00:24:09,860
Maybe it just helps to see your last attempt.

310
00:24:10,446 --> 00:24:11,584
So they tested that.

311
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:17,316
The pink dashed line gives the model its previous attempt but no reflection, and it helps a little.

312
00:24:17,742 --> 00:24:23,332
The dark purple line adds the written reflection on top, and it helps clearly more.

313
00:24:23,726 --> 00:24:30,468
So the value is in the diagnosis, the sentence that says what went wrong, not only in seeing the old attempt.

314
00:24:30,926 --> 00:24:35,844
For your own agents, when a step fails, don't just retry the same prompt.

315
00:24:36,270 --> 00:24:42,276
Ask the model to write one or two sentences about why it failed and put those in the next attempt.

316
00:24:43,374 --> 00:24:45,284
Now here's the catch, I promise.

317
00:24:45,902 --> 00:24:49,732
Remember that every reflection example had an outside signal.

318
00:24:50,094 --> 00:24:55,664
What happens when there isn't one, and you just ask the model, check your own answer.

319
00:24:56,046 --> 00:24:59,172
This paper from Google DeepMind looked at exactly that.

320
00:24:59,662 --> 00:25:04,644
Its title says it plainly: large language models cannot self-correct reasoning yet.

321
00:25:04,974 --> 00:25:06,276
Look at the table.

322
00:25:06,734 --> 00:25:11,780
The top row is GBT4 Turbo answering math word problems and common sense questions once.

323
00:25:12,302 --> 00:25:15,748
The rows below ask it to review its answer and fix any problems.

324
00:25:16,334 --> 00:25:18,212
And the scores mostly go down.

325
00:25:18,606 --> 00:25:23,300
On the common sense questions, it drops by up to almost 10 points with one of the prompts.

326
00:25:23,822 --> 00:25:26,180
Why would checking your work make you worse?

327
00:25:26,606 --> 00:25:29,124
Because the model has no new information.

328
00:25:29,710 --> 00:25:34,468
It's the same model, reading the same question so it has the same blind spots.

329
00:25:35,118 --> 00:25:36,384
Asked, are you sure?

330
00:25:36,686 --> 00:25:39,332
It often talks itself out of a right answer.

331
00:25:39,726 --> 00:25:41,604
And there's a fairness problem too.

332
00:25:42,350 --> 00:25:52,224
Some earlier papers that reported big self-correction gains were using the correct answer to decide when to stop correcting, which is information you don't have in real use.

333
00:25:52,878 --> 00:25:57,892
That includes reflection's trivia result, where the zero came straight from the answer key.

334
00:25:58,382 --> 00:26:01,988
So reflection works because the environment gives it real feedback.

335
00:26:02,446 --> 00:26:05,504
Take that away, and much of the benefit goes with it.

336
00:26:05,902 --> 00:26:08,064
One update, and it's on the slide.

337
00:26:08,462 --> 00:26:14,500
This finding came before today's reasoning models, the ones trained to think for a long time before answering.

338
00:26:14,926 --> 00:26:20,260
Those models do catch and fix their own mistakes while they think, because they were trained to.

339
00:26:20,686 --> 00:26:25,284
So in practice, you don't usually build a separate now critique yourself step anymore.

340
00:26:25,742 --> 00:26:27,844
That's mostly inside the model now.

341
00:26:28,526 --> 00:26:33,892
What you still have to build is the outside signal, the tests, the checks, the tool results.

342
00:26:35,022 --> 00:26:37,092
Which brings us to critic.

343
00:26:37,518 --> 00:26:42,096
The idea if self-critique fails because the model has no new information.

344
00:26:42,510 --> 00:26:44,964
Give the critic tools so it can go get some.

345
00:26:45,358 --> 00:26:46,976
Look at the loop on the left.

346
00:26:47,342 --> 00:26:53,828
The model writes an answer, then a verify step checks it using outside tools, which you can see in the middle box.

347
00:26:54,222 --> 00:26:58,084
Search, a calculator, a code interpreter, Wikipedia.

348
00:26:58,638 --> 00:27:02,852
That produces a critique, the model corrects its answer and the loop runs again.

349
00:27:03,278 --> 00:27:04,868
Now the chart on the right.

350
00:27:05,198 --> 00:27:10,084
The orange triangles are critic with tools and it jumps up after the first round.

351
00:27:10,414 --> 00:27:17,956
The dotted line with the X marks is the same loop with the tools taken away, so the model is critiquing itself from memory.

352
00:27:18,478 --> 00:27:19,748
It barely moves.

353
00:27:20,270 --> 00:27:23,716
Same loop, same model, and the difference is entirely the tools.

354
00:27:24,270 --> 00:27:33,668
In our buzz briefing, this is the difference between asking the model is that charging cost right, and letting it search for the utility's current rate and compare.

355
00:27:34,062 --> 00:27:36,964
The second one can actually catch the old number.

356
00:27:37,486 --> 00:27:40,356
So here's the practical rule from this whole section.

357
00:27:40,814 --> 00:27:44,964
If you want a checker in your system, make the checker an agent too.

358
00:27:45,390 --> 00:27:47,844
Give it tools to look things up or run code.

359
00:27:48,238 --> 00:27:52,484
A checker with no tools can only repeat what the writer already believed.

360
00:27:52,846 --> 00:27:56,228
And notice the top line on the chart, the Oracle version.

361
00:27:56,622 --> 00:28:01,796
That's critic when it's allowed to peek at the right answer to decide when to stop.

362
00:28:02,158 --> 00:28:10,436
It does even better, which tells you again how much a stopping signal is worth and why you shouldn't trust results that quietly use one.

363
00:28:11,534 --> 00:28:12,548
A new question.

364
00:28:13,070 --> 00:28:16,896
When the agent wants to do something, what should the action actually look like?

365
00:28:17,422 --> 00:28:20,260
Codec's answer is that the action should be code.

366
00:28:21,390 --> 00:28:23,076
First, the usual way.

367
00:28:23,630 --> 00:28:29,604
Most agents act by producing a small piece of structured text that names the tool and its arguments.

368
00:28:30,094 --> 00:28:37,376
That format is JSON, which is just curly braces with names and values, like tool, lookup rates, country, Germany.

369
00:28:37,582 --> 00:28:39,136
Look at the task in the blue box.

370
00:28:39,696 --> 00:28:43,588
Find the cheapest country to buy a phone in out of four countries.

371
00:28:44,110 --> 00:28:46,372
Now look at how many steps that takes.

372
00:28:47,022 --> 00:28:49,668
Look up the exchange rate for Germany, wait.

373
00:28:50,382 --> 00:28:52,896
Look up the phone price in Germany, wait.

374
00:28:53,540 --> 00:28:58,276
Convert and add tax, wait, then shipping, then the final price.

375
00:28:58,862 --> 00:29:00,100
That's one country.

376
00:29:00,590 --> 00:29:05,060
Then the agent does all of it again for Japan, the USA, and India.

377
00:29:05,614 --> 00:29:08,484
Every one of those steps is a full round trip.

378
00:29:09,134 --> 00:29:19,172
The model writes one call, stops, the system runs it, pastes the result into the prompt, and the model reads the whole growing history again to write the next call.

379
00:29:19,566 --> 00:29:22,212
It's slow and it calls tokens on every step.

380
00:29:22,734 --> 00:29:30,212
And the model has to keep track of all the in-between numbers in the text, which is exactly where it can slip and copy the wrong one.

381
00:29:31,310 --> 00:29:34,116
Now the same task with code as the action.

382
00:29:34,638 --> 00:29:36,164
Look at the code block.

383
00:29:36,494 --> 00:29:38,948
The agent writes one short Python program.

384
00:29:39,470 --> 00:29:46,692
It's a for loop over the four countries, calling the same five tools as plain functions, saving each price into a dictionary.

385
00:29:47,086 --> 00:29:49,060
Then one line picks the minimum.

386
00:29:49,582 --> 00:29:52,176
It runs once and the answer comes back Japan.

387
00:29:52,750 --> 00:29:57,348
Look at the two green notes on the slide because there are the two reasons this wins.

388
00:29:57,902 --> 00:29:59,456
First, control flow.

389
00:29:59,950 --> 00:30:03,536
A loop and an if statement are things code already has.

390
00:30:03,950 --> 00:30:08,932
With JSON, the model has to act out, the loop by hand, one call at a time.

391
00:30:09,326 --> 00:30:10,692
Second, reuse.

392
00:30:11,278 --> 00:30:14,212
That mind function comes for free from Python.

393
00:30:14,734 --> 00:30:20,196
With JSON tools, if nobody wrote a find the minimum tool, the model has to do it in its head.

394
00:30:20,846 --> 00:30:23,556
There's one more reason that isn't on the slide.

395
00:30:24,142 --> 00:30:27,108
Models have seen a huge amount of code during training.

396
00:30:27,566 --> 00:30:31,076
Writing a for loop in Python is a very familiar task for them.

397
00:30:31,502 --> 00:30:36,036
Writing a long series of custom JSON calls is a format they've seen much less of.

398
00:30:36,462 --> 00:30:37,732
Back to our briefing.

399
00:30:38,094 --> 00:30:44,868
The assistant needs the cost per bus over 12 years for four bus makers, with fuel, charging, and maintenance.

400
00:30:45,376 --> 00:30:48,324
As JSON calls, that's dozens of round trips.

401
00:30:48,814 --> 00:30:54,724
As code is one small program and arithmetic is done by the computer instead of by the model.

402
00:30:55,086 --> 00:31:01,892
Now the catch, and it's a serious one, is the agents can run any code, it can do anything code can do.

403
00:31:02,318 --> 00:31:06,976
It can delete files, read your secrets, or send data out to the internet.

404
00:31:07,566 --> 00:31:14,096
So cult actions need a sandbox, meaning an isolated environment where the code can't touch anything that matters.

405
00:31:14,414 --> 00:31:19,300
We'll come back to this at the end of today with an agent that has no protections at all.

406
00:31:20,302 --> 00:31:21,776
Does it actually help?

407
00:31:22,158 --> 00:31:23,300
Look at the left chart.

408
00:31:23,790 --> 00:31:27,044
Each row is a model, and the green bar is code as aption.

409
00:31:27,376 --> 00:31:32,932
For most models, especially the strong ones at the top, the green bar is the longest.

410
00:31:33,358 --> 00:31:41,348
For the best model there, GPT-4, it's around three quarters of tasks solved with code versus about half with JSON or plain text.

411
00:31:41,678 --> 00:31:45,252
The right chart is the number of turns and shorter is better.

412
00:31:45,710 --> 00:31:47,300
Doing is usually shortest.

413
00:31:48,078 --> 00:31:52,068
So code actions get more tasks right and use fewer steps to do it.

414
00:31:52,462 --> 00:31:54,180
Notice the bottom rows, though.

415
00:31:54,734 --> 00:31:56,644
The weaker models gain much less.

416
00:31:57,326 --> 00:32:00,932
Code actions help most when the model is already good at writing code.

417
00:32:01,262 --> 00:32:09,796
And this is a big reason why so many agents today, especially coding agents, have a tool like run this in the terminal or run this Python.

418
00:32:10,158 --> 00:32:12,772
It's the Code Act idea, used in practice.

419
00:32:13,262 --> 00:32:21,220
The paper tested 17 models in total and reports up to 20% higher success rates with code actions than with the alternatives.

420
00:32:22,478 --> 00:32:24,996
Next button, Plan First, then execute.

421
00:32:25,454 --> 00:32:27,652
Look at the key idea at the bottom.

422
00:32:28,110 --> 00:32:32,228
Planning lets you turn an agent's actions back into a workflow.

423
00:32:32,622 --> 00:32:35,684
The agent writes the workflow and then playing code runs it.

424
00:32:36,110 --> 00:32:38,532
There's a second benefit that's easy to miss.

425
00:32:38,958 --> 00:32:44,484
Once the plan is written down before anything runs, a person or a program can look at it first.

426
00:32:44,878 --> 00:32:49,604
You can check it, approve it, or reject it before the agent touches anything.

427
00:32:49,966 --> 00:32:55,524
That's much harder to do with React, where the next step doesn't exist until the previous one finishes.

428
00:32:56,718 --> 00:33:00,452
This is Rewool, which stands for reasoning without observation.

429
00:33:00,878 --> 00:33:02,148
Look at the three boxes.

430
00:33:02,798 --> 00:33:06,788
The planner, top left, writes out the whole plan before anything runs.

431
00:33:07,278 --> 00:33:09,956
Notice the labels E1, E2, and so on.

432
00:33:10,510 --> 00:33:15,268
Each step stores its results in the name slot, and later steps refer to those slots.

433
00:33:15,630 --> 00:33:21,124
Step 2 says, find the main ingredient given E1, even though E1 doesn't exist yet.

434
00:33:21,582 --> 00:33:26,276
Then the worker on the right runs each step in order and fills in the slots.

435
00:33:26,606 --> 00:33:32,644
Finally, the solver at the bottom reads the plan plus all the evidence and writes the answer.

436
00:33:33,038 --> 00:33:34,052
Why bother?

437
00:33:34,574 --> 00:33:37,156
Think about what React does on every step.

438
00:33:37,614 --> 00:33:44,336
It sends the whole growing history back to the model, the instructions, the examples, every thought, every search result.

439
00:33:44,814 --> 00:33:46,564
That's a lot of repeated tokens.

440
00:33:47,182 --> 00:33:50,596
Rewool calls the planning model once, and then the steps are cheap.

441
00:33:51,182 --> 00:33:53,700
Some of them don't need a big model at all.

442
00:33:54,798 --> 00:33:56,292
And here's the payoff.

443
00:33:56,782 --> 00:34:02,976
The y-axis is accuracy, and the x-axis is tokens used, so you want to be up and to the left.

444
00:34:03,342 --> 00:34:06,436
The red arrows go from reac to re wool.

445
00:34:06,958 --> 00:34:11,844
On both benchmarks, rewool is more accurate and uses far fewer tokens.

446
00:34:12,494 --> 00:34:19,856
The paper reports about 5 times better token efficiency and around 4 points higher accuracy on Hotpot QA.

447
00:34:20,302 --> 00:34:21,444
Now the catch.

448
00:34:21,998 --> 00:34:26,468
Rew all pants without seeing any results, which is right there in the name.

449
00:34:27,022 --> 00:34:31,076
If step 1 comes back with something surprising, the plan can't adapt.

450
00:34:31,406 --> 00:34:41,936
For our buzz briefing, if the plan says look up the price of the Model X bus, and it turns out that model was discontinued, RWU keeps going with a broken plan.

451
00:34:42,382 --> 00:34:45,188
React would have noticed and changed course.

452
00:34:46,382 --> 00:34:51,556
LLM compiler from Berkeley fixes both of Ryuo's weak spots.

453
00:34:52,046 --> 00:35:00,016
The name comes from Compilers, the programs that turn your code into machine instructions and figure out what can run at the same time.

454
00:35:00,398 --> 00:35:02,948
Look at the planner's output in the middle box.

455
00:35:03,374 --> 00:35:09,136
It's a list of tasks with dependencies, which the slide calls a DAG, a directed acyclic graph.

456
00:35:09,582 --> 00:35:15,652
BACTS means a set of steps with arrows showing which steps need which results and no loops.

457
00:35:16,334 --> 00:35:18,692
Search Microsoft's market cap search apples.

458
00:35:19,182 --> 00:35:20,868
Neither depends on the other.

459
00:35:21,358 --> 00:35:22,852
The division depends on both.

460
00:35:23,310 --> 00:35:29,956
Then a task fetching unit sends out any task whose inputs are ready and the executor runs them in parallel.

461
00:35:30,542 --> 00:35:33,892
The two differences from Bri Wu are on the slide.

462
00:35:34,254 --> 00:35:41,092
Independent steps run at the same time, and if results come back that change things, the planner can replan.

463
00:35:42,222 --> 00:35:44,804
Here's what parallel execution buys you.

464
00:35:45,262 --> 00:35:48,996
The question asks whether two filmmakers have the same nationality.

465
00:35:49,358 --> 00:35:54,212
React on the left searches for the first person, waits, thinks.

466
00:35:54,766 --> 00:35:58,212
Searches for the second, waits, and then answers.

467
00:35:58,574 --> 00:36:05,220
LLM compiler on the right sees that the two searches are independent and runs them at the same time.

468
00:36:05,646 --> 00:36:06,384
Same answer.

469
00:36:06,544 --> 00:36:10,020
About 1.8 times faster on this kind of question.

470
00:36:10,414 --> 00:36:15,876
For our briefing, looking up four busmaker's prices is exactly this situation.

471
00:36:16,302 --> 00:36:19,332
There is no reason to do them one after another.

472
00:36:20,462 --> 00:36:21,348
The full results.

473
00:36:22,158 --> 00:36:28,224
I want you to look at one row in particular, the one labeled OAI Parallel Function.

474
00:36:28,590 --> 00:36:36,036
That's OpenAI's built-in parallel function calling, where the model can ask for several tool calls in a single turn.

475
00:36:36,494 --> 00:36:39,332
It already gets a good part of the speedup on its own.

476
00:36:39,790 --> 00:36:42,628
That tells you where this idea ended up.

477
00:36:43,086 --> 00:36:50,212
Today, most model APIs let the model request several tool calls at once, and the harness runs them in parallel.

478
00:36:50,574 --> 00:36:53,668
So, you mostly don't need a separate compiler anymore.

479
00:36:54,030 --> 00:36:56,708
The idea moved into the model and the API.

480
00:36:57,038 --> 00:37:07,236
The paper reports up to about 3.7 times faster and up to about 6.7 times cheaper than React with similar or better accuracy.

481
00:37:08,270 --> 00:37:09,764
Let's recap this section.

482
00:37:11,054 --> 00:37:15,588
React reasoning before acting is useful, and it's still the standard loop.

483
00:37:16,064 --> 00:37:16,864
Reflection.

484
00:37:17,166 --> 00:37:23,876
Self-reflection mostly happens inside the model's reasoning today, and the part you still build is the outside feedback.

485
00:37:24,238 --> 00:37:24,784
Code Act.

486
00:37:25,198 --> 00:37:27,044
Code is a good format for actions.

487
00:37:27,566 --> 00:37:30,464
Re-wool and LLM compiler.

488
00:37:30,624 --> 00:37:36,452
The parallel part now mostly lives in parallel tool calls, but planning is still important.

489
00:37:36,974 --> 00:37:44,868
And sometimes people still use a fixed workflow to coordinate sub-agents, which brings us full circle to where we started today.

490
00:37:45,998 --> 00:37:47,684
Now context management.

491
00:37:48,398 --> 00:37:51,940
This is where a lot of the real engineering in agents happens.

492
00:37:53,230 --> 00:37:58,596
Same agent loop, but now look at the green box around the model call, the context manager.

493
00:37:58,958 --> 00:38:03,984
Every time the loop goes around, something has to decide what goes into the prompt for the next call.

494
00:38:04,366 --> 00:38:07,620
In the simplest agent, the answer is everything so far.

495
00:38:08,046 --> 00:38:09,348
That works for 10 steps.

496
00:38:09,902 --> 00:38:11,492
It fails at 200.

497
00:38:12,014 --> 00:38:14,784
Picture our briefing agent after an hour of work.

498
00:38:15,150 --> 00:38:23,744
It has read six busmaker brochures, two utility rate sheets, a transit union contract, and the minutes of three council meetings.

499
00:38:24,046 --> 00:38:28,644
It has run a dozen cost calculations, and some of them it threw away.

500
00:38:28,974 --> 00:38:33,380
If all of that stays in the prompt, every new step pays to reread it.

501
00:38:33,774 --> 00:38:40,464
And the one sentence that matters, the council rejects diesel hybrids, is buried somewhere in the middle.

502
00:38:41,038 --> 00:38:46,372
This picture is from Anthropic's context engineering post, which was another lecture to reading.

503
00:38:46,702 --> 00:38:51,876
On the left is Prompt Engineering, where you write one system prompt and one user message.

504
00:38:52,206 --> 00:38:54,436
On the right is context engineering.

505
00:38:55,022 --> 00:39:01,860
There's a big pool of things you could include documents, tools, memory files, instructions, message history.

506
00:39:02,190 --> 00:39:05,924
And at every step you pick which of them actually go into the window.

507
00:39:06,318 --> 00:39:13,956
The post defines it as finding the smallest possible set of high signal tokens that gives you the outcome you want.

508
00:39:14,446 --> 00:39:17,476
HighSignal just means actually useful for the current step.

509
00:39:17,902 --> 00:39:21,732
The post also describes something it calls just in time context.

510
00:39:22,126 --> 00:39:32,704
Instead of loading a whole document up front, the agent keeps a short reference, like a file path or a link, and only reads the document with a tool when it actually needs it.

511
00:39:33,104 --> 00:39:35,268
Coding agents work this way.

512
00:39:35,854 --> 00:39:41,956
They don't paste your whole repository into the prompt, they search it and open files as they go.

513
00:39:43,214 --> 00:39:44,740
Why does this matter?

514
00:39:45,198 --> 00:39:52,720
This is the needle in a haystack test that Greg Comrad ran on Claude 2.1 in late 2023.

515
00:39:53,198 --> 00:40:00,960
He hid one sentence in a long document and asked the model to find it, varying how long the document was and where the sentence sat.

516
00:40:01,326 --> 00:40:04,516
Green means it found it, red means it didn't.

517
00:40:04,974 --> 00:40:09,520
As you go right toward 100,000 tokens, there's more and more red.

518
00:40:10,030 --> 00:40:11,652
There's a follow-up I like.

519
00:40:12,174 --> 00:40:26,800
Anthropic responded that the model was reluctant to answer from one out-of-place sentence, and that adding one line to the prompt, here is the most relevant sentence in the context, raised accuracy on this test from 27 to 98%.

520
00:40:28,078 --> 00:40:30,788
So some of the failure was about prompting.

521
00:40:31,214 --> 00:40:33,760
But the general effect is real and well documented.

522
00:40:34,126 --> 00:40:37,120
Anthropic's own post calls it context rot.

523
00:40:37,582 --> 00:40:42,276
The more tokens you put in, the worse the model gets at using any single one of them.

524
00:40:42,702 --> 00:40:46,820
Remember Lost in the Middle from Lecture 1, which found the same thing.

525
00:40:47,310 --> 00:40:49,988
A bigger window doesn't mean you should fill it.

526
00:40:51,118 --> 00:40:52,548
So there are two principles.

527
00:40:53,102 --> 00:40:54,436
Look at the two columns.

528
00:40:55,054 --> 00:40:57,284
The first is to focus the context.

529
00:40:57,838 --> 00:40:59,812
Keep only what the current step needs.

530
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:05,700
The full history doesn't have to be in the window, and extra stuff is just bloat.

531
00:41:06,254 --> 00:41:08,516
The second is to preserve continuity.

532
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:15,200
Whatever you cut, the agent still has to remember the goal, the constraints, and what it has already done.

533
00:41:15,630 --> 00:41:20,676
If you drop the city council said no diesel hybrids the agent might happily recommend one.

534
00:41:21,070 --> 00:41:24,644
And there's a practical point in the last bullet, the KV cache.

535
00:41:25,038 --> 00:41:33,956
When a model reads a prompt, it stores intermediate results for every token, so if the next call starts with the same tokens, it can skip rereading them.

536
00:41:34,382 --> 00:41:37,476
Providers charge much less for those cached tokens.

537
00:41:38,094 --> 00:41:41,828
But the cache only works if the beginning of the prompt stays exactly the same.

538
00:41:42,318 --> 00:41:48,612
So if you rewrite old parts of the context every turn, you lose the cache and pay full price every time.

539
00:41:49,006 --> 00:41:53,188
Good context management changes the end of the prompt and leaves the beginning alone.

540
00:41:54,350 --> 00:41:56,324
So let's go through the patterns.

541
00:41:57,774 --> 00:42:05,380
There are five on this slide: a persistent event log, memory offloading, compaction, observation masking, and context isolation.

542
00:42:05,710 --> 00:42:08,000
We'll take each one with a real system.

543
00:42:08,718 --> 00:42:12,400
Here's the 1.9 version of each before we look closer.

544
00:42:12,846 --> 00:42:18,788
The event log keeps a full record somewhere outside the prompt, so you can rebuild the prompt any way you like.

545
00:42:19,150 --> 00:42:23,204
Memory offloading saves important facts on where you can search later.

546
00:42:23,598 --> 00:42:26,436
Compaction replaces old history with a summary.

547
00:42:26,766 --> 00:42:29,700
Observation masking hides old tool outputs.

548
00:42:30,094 --> 00:42:34,340
And isolation gives separate jobs their own separate context windows.

549
00:42:35,534 --> 00:42:38,692
First, the event log, using OpenHands as the example.

550
00:42:39,150 --> 00:42:45,284
OpenHands, which used to be called OpenDevon, is an open source platform for coding agents.

551
00:42:45,998 --> 00:42:48,964
Look at the big middle panel, the event stream.

552
00:42:49,358 --> 00:42:52,612
Everything that happens is recorded as an event in order.

553
00:42:53,070 --> 00:43:01,156
The user's message, the agent writing a file, the result that write, the agent starting a server, the browser output.

554
00:43:01,550 --> 00:43:03,908
Each action produces an observation.

555
00:43:04,398 --> 00:43:12,708
On the right, actions actually run inside a Docker sandbox, which is an isolated container, so the agent can't damage the host machine.

556
00:43:13,774 --> 00:43:15,680
Here's the key design choice.

557
00:43:16,110 --> 00:43:19,780
The event log is the source of truth and it's append only.

558
00:43:20,174 --> 00:43:25,316
That means you only ever add to the end and never edit or delete old entries.

559
00:43:25,934 --> 00:43:29,540
Then, every time you call the model, you build a prompt from the log.

560
00:43:29,870 --> 00:43:31,556
Look at the pipeline at the top.

561
00:43:32,046 --> 00:43:38,596
Take the event list, filter to the events the model should see, group related ones, and convert them to messages.

562
00:43:39,086 --> 00:43:41,348
Why separate the log from the prompt?

563
00:43:41,742 --> 00:43:45,508
Because now you can change what the model sees without losing anything.

564
00:43:45,934 --> 00:43:51,396
You can summarize, truncate, or hide things in the prompt, and the full record is still there.

565
00:43:51,758 --> 00:44:01,764
You can debug a run by replaying the log, and other programs can watch the log, like a monitor that alerts you when the agent tries to run a dangerous command.

566
00:44:02,990 --> 00:44:06,020
Second, memory offloading with MemGPT.

567
00:44:06,350 --> 00:44:09,444
We'll go deep on this on Wednesday, so here's the short version.

568
00:44:09,838 --> 00:44:11,492
Look at the left side.

569
00:44:12,046 --> 00:44:17,808
The system warns that memory is getting full, and the agent responds by saving the important facts.

570
00:44:18,094 --> 00:44:24,356
Birthday is February 7 and boyfriend named James to a storage area outside the conversation.

571
00:44:24,846 --> 00:44:27,780
Then the old messages can be dropped from the prompt.

572
00:44:28,110 --> 00:44:34,660
On the right, later on it searches its stored history for six blacks and pulls back exactly the relevant lines.

573
00:44:35,310 --> 00:44:46,308
For our briefing, the agent might save council rejects diesel hybrids, and budget cap is 40 million to a notes file, and then let the long meeting transcript fall out of the prompt.

574
00:44:47,374 --> 00:44:48,804
Third, compaction.

575
00:44:49,326 --> 00:44:50,884
Look at the two columns.

576
00:44:51,406 --> 00:44:57,540
On the left is the raw agent, three tons of reasoning, action, and observation all stacked up.

577
00:44:57,934 --> 00:45:05,124
On the right, the first two turns have been replaced by one summary written by the model and only the latest turn is kept in full.

578
00:45:05,486 --> 00:45:06,692
Clog code does this.

579
00:45:07,310 --> 00:45:16,288
A context engineering post says it keeps things like architectural decisions and unresolved bugs, plus the five files the agent touched most recently.

580
00:45:16,686 --> 00:45:20,708
The slide's last bullet says it can help for compaction itself to be agentic.

581
00:45:21,070 --> 00:45:28,388
That means letting the model decide what's worth keeping, maybe even looking things up rather than running one fix summarize this prompt.

582
00:45:28,814 --> 00:45:34,180
The catch is that a summary loses things and you don't know what it lost until the agent needs it.

583
00:45:34,670 --> 00:45:43,076
Both COD code and pie, which we'll see in a few minutes, do this automatically when the window gets close to full, and you can also trigger it by hand.

584
00:45:44,814 --> 00:45:46,276
Fourth, observation masking.

585
00:45:46,638 --> 00:45:52,128
Look at this pie chart from a 2025 paper called the Complexity Trap.

586
00:45:52,366 --> 00:45:55,460
It shows where the tokens go in a coding agent's history.

587
00:45:55,950 --> 00:45:57,476
Reasoning is under 10%.

588
00:45:58,688 --> 00:46:00,288
Actions are under 7.

589
00:46:01,038 --> 00:46:08,128
Observations, meaning tool outputs like file contents, test logs, and search results, are about 84%.

590
00:46:09,102 --> 00:46:12,356
So the stuff filling up the context isn't the agent's thinking.

591
00:46:12,814 --> 00:46:13,968
It's the raw output.

592
00:46:13,980 --> 00:46:15,600
it's read along the way.

593
00:46:16,782 --> 00:46:18,980
The idea is very simple.

594
00:46:19,374 --> 00:46:28,708
Instead of asking a model to summarize old history, just replace old tool outputs with a short placeholder, something like output omitted.

595
00:46:29,326 --> 00:46:34,916
Keep the agent's own reasoning and actions and keep the most recent observations in full.

596
00:46:35,374 --> 00:46:43,108
It's especially useful for coding agents, where a single test run or file read can be thousands of lines.

597
00:46:43,630 --> 00:46:47,172
For our briefing, it's the 40-page BuzzMaker PDF.

598
00:46:47,566 --> 00:46:49,380
The agent read 20 steps ago.

599
00:46:49,934 --> 00:46:52,196
It already wrote down what it needed.

600
00:46:52,590 --> 00:46:54,692
It doesn't need the whole PDF anymore.

601
00:46:55,214 --> 00:46:57,248
The catch is small, but real.

602
00:46:57,838 --> 00:47:02,852
If the agent later needs something from a MASP output, it has to run the tool again.

603
00:47:03,438 --> 00:47:06,404
Usually that's cheap, like reopening a file.

604
00:47:06,734 --> 00:47:15,588
But if the tool was slow or costly, like a long test suite or a paid search, you might keep a short note of the result before you hide it.

605
00:47:16,814 --> 00:47:18,368
And here's the surprise.

606
00:47:18,830 --> 00:47:26,692
The paper ran SWE Agent, an open source coding agent, on SWE Bench Verified, a set of real GitHub bug fixes.

607
00:47:27,310 --> 00:47:29,648
Each chart is a different model.

608
00:47:30,158 --> 00:47:33,088
The black dot is the raw agent with full history.

609
00:47:33,422 --> 00:47:36,580
The pink square is LLM summarization.

610
00:47:36,942 --> 00:47:39,300
The purple triangle is plain masking.

611
00:47:39,886 --> 00:47:43,300
You won't up and to the left, meaning more soft for less money.

612
00:47:43,726 --> 00:47:53,028
In most cases, masking is about as good as summarization, sometimes slightly better, and at roughly half the cost compared to the raw agent.

613
00:47:53,390 --> 00:47:59,076
The middle chart is the exception where the raw agent solves more but at more than twice the price.

614
00:47:59,566 --> 00:48:01,828
The paper's point is in its title.

615
00:48:02,510 --> 00:48:05,572
The simple method often matches the complicated one.

616
00:48:06,030 --> 00:48:10,660
So try the cheap, simple thing first and measure before you build the clever one.

617
00:48:11,886 --> 00:48:14,116
Fifth, context isolation.

618
00:48:14,606 --> 00:48:21,732
A planner splits the work and hands each piece to a subagent, and each sub-agent has its own separate context window.

619
00:48:22,158 --> 00:48:27,684
A subagent might read 50 pages about one busmaker, but it returns only a short summary.

620
00:48:28,110 --> 00:48:32,932
Anthropics Post says these summaries are often 1 to 2,000 tokens.

621
00:48:33,582 --> 00:48:36,228
The main agent never sees the 50 pages.

622
00:48:36,718 --> 00:48:40,932
This is orchestrator workers from earlier, but used for a different reason.

623
00:48:41,326 --> 00:48:44,004
Before, the reason was spitting up the work.

624
00:48:44,398 --> 00:48:47,364
Here the reason is keeping each context clean.

625
00:48:47,726 --> 00:49:00,836
The catch is that the sub-agents can't see each other's work, so if two of them need to agree on an assumption, like what year's electricity price to use, you have to pass that to both of them explicitly.

626
00:49:01,966 --> 00:49:04,624
A related idea is progressive disclosure.

627
00:49:05,070 --> 00:49:08,900
You don't need every tool and every instruction in the first prompt.

628
00:49:09,454 --> 00:49:13,092
You show a little up front and reveal more when it's needed.

629
00:49:13,422 --> 00:49:15,012
Look at the skill file.

630
00:49:15,770 --> 00:49:19,972
Antropic introduced these agent skills in October 2025.

631
00:49:20,398 --> 00:49:24,516
At the top is a short header with just a name and a one-line description.

632
00:49:24,846 --> 00:49:28,064
Only that header goes into the agent's prompt at the start.

633
00:49:28,462 --> 00:49:32,676
If the agent decides it needs to work with a PDF, it reads the rest of the file.

634
00:49:33,070 --> 00:49:39,588
And the file points to more files, like a forms guide that it reads only if it actually needs them.

635
00:49:40,142 --> 00:49:46,468
So you can give an agent 100 skills and pay for only a few lines each until one gets used.

636
00:49:47,438 --> 00:49:49,584
And the note on this slide is important.

637
00:49:50,222 --> 00:49:52,624
These strategies all work together.

638
00:49:53,038 --> 00:49:55,904
You can save facts to memory and then compact.

639
00:49:56,430 --> 00:50:00,196
You can build the prompt from an event log and also mask old observations.

640
00:50:00,782 --> 00:50:03,908
Real systems use several of them, sometimes all five.

641
00:50:04,462 --> 00:50:07,504
Here's how our briefing agent might use all five.

642
00:50:07,854 --> 00:50:10,704
Every action and result goes into an event log.

643
00:50:11,246 --> 00:50:16,836
Key facts, like the council's constraints and the budget cap, get saved to a notes file.

644
00:50:17,294 --> 00:50:21,508
Old brochure text gets masked once agent has pulled out the numbers.

645
00:50:21,966 --> 00:50:28,004
One sub agent per bus maker does the reading in its own context and sends back a short summary.

646
00:50:28,398 --> 00:50:32,356
And if the main conversation still gets long, compaction kicks in.

647
00:50:32,718 --> 00:50:38,852
Each piece is simple, and together they let the agent work for hours without drowning in its own history.

648
00:50:39,950 --> 00:50:40,868
Let's recap.

649
00:50:42,190 --> 00:50:46,052
Here's another way to organize all of this from a long chain post.

650
00:50:46,574 --> 00:50:49,664
Four verbs write, select, compress, and isolate.

651
00:50:50,062 --> 00:50:55,236
Write means saving context somewhere outside the window, like a scratch pad or long-term memory.

652
00:50:55,790 --> 00:51:00,612
Select means pulling the right pieces back in, like the right tools or the right memories.

653
00:51:01,134 --> 00:51:03,460
Compress means summarizing or trimming.

654
00:51:04,078 --> 00:51:08,516
Isolate means splitting context across sandboxes, state or multiple agents.

655
00:51:09,070 --> 00:51:12,004
Everything we covered fits into one of these boxes.

656
00:51:12,526 --> 00:51:15,620
You can line up today's five patterns with these verbs.

657
00:51:16,110 --> 00:51:19,300
The event log and memory offloading are ways to write.

658
00:51:19,630 --> 00:51:22,980
Progressive disclosure and searching memory are ways to select.

659
00:51:23,344 --> 00:51:26,756
Compaction and observation masking are ways to compress.

660
00:51:27,150 --> 00:51:29,860
And subagents are a way to isolate.

661
00:51:30,926 --> 00:51:33,060
And the circle pieces are coming up.

662
00:51:33,550 --> 00:51:37,668
Long-term memory, both writing and retrieving it, is this Wednesday.

663
00:51:38,414 --> 00:51:41,540
Splitting work across multiple agents is next Monday.

664
00:51:42,784 --> 00:51:48,292
So event logs, memory, compaction, observation masking, and isolation are the key context patterns.

665
00:51:48,870 --> 00:51:50,628
Real systems Combine them.

666
00:51:51,150 --> 00:51:53,220
And all of them come from two principles.

667
00:51:53,486 --> 00:51:56,772
Keep the context focused and preserve continuity.

668
00:51:57,950 --> 00:52:00,830
Let's look at a real harness and spot these decisions.

669
00:52:02,144 --> 00:52:05,024
This is Pi, a minimal coding agent harness.

670
00:52:05,646 --> 00:52:08,224
It's open source under the MIT license.

671
00:52:09,710 --> 00:52:11,104
Here's its philosophy.

672
00:52:11,310 --> 00:52:18,980
In its own words, Pi is a minimal agent harness, and you adapt Pi to your workflows, not the other way around.

673
00:52:19,566 --> 00:52:21,604
So look at the list of what's missing.

674
00:52:22,222 --> 00:52:28,868
No subagents, no permission checks, no plan mode, no to-do lists, and no background shell commands.

675
00:52:29,454 --> 00:52:31,904
Popular harnesses ship all of those.

676
00:52:32,366 --> 00:52:39,076
Pi leaves them out on purpose and lets you add them yourself with extensions, which are small TypeScript plugins.

677
00:52:39,470 --> 00:52:41,028
People have built a lot of them.

678
00:52:41,710 --> 00:52:46,308
Pi stocks also point to OpenCloud as an example of a real project built on top of it.

679
00:52:46,702 --> 00:52:48,164
Why build it that way?

680
00:52:48,590 --> 00:52:53,744
Every built-in feature adds instructions and tools to the prompt, whether or not you're using it.

681
00:52:54,126 --> 00:52:59,108
A minimal core keeps the context small and you only add what your workflow needs.

682
00:53:00,302 --> 00:53:01,668
Here are the tools.

683
00:53:02,126 --> 00:53:04,464
By default, all the four are turned on.

684
00:53:04,750 --> 00:53:08,740
Read a file, write a file, edit a file, and run a shell command.

685
00:53:09,230 --> 00:53:16,708
The others in the table, like repfind and ls, are really just things you could do through the shell anyway.

686
00:53:17,198 --> 00:53:19,492
This connects straight back to Codact.

687
00:53:19,918 --> 00:53:24,676
If the agent has a shell, it has every command line program ever written.

688
00:53:25,134 --> 00:53:31,268
You don't need a hundred custom JSON tools, and fewer tool descriptions means a smaller prompt.

689
00:53:31,662 --> 00:53:33,956
That's the bloat point on the slide.

690
00:53:35,086 --> 00:53:37,104
The loop is plain Rhea.

691
00:53:37,710 --> 00:53:41,988
The model reasons then makes JSON tool calls, gets results, and repeats.

692
00:53:42,734 --> 00:53:44,772
The interesting part is the picture.

693
00:53:45,326 --> 00:53:48,356
The conversation is stored as a tree, not a list.

694
00:53:48,654 --> 00:53:53,264
At any point you can go back to an earlier message and start a new branch from there.

695
00:53:53,584 --> 00:53:56,932
Say your agent went down a back path for 10 steps.

696
00:53:57,326 --> 00:54:04,944
Instead of arguing with it, you jump back to before it went wrong and try again, and the bad branch is still saved if you want it.

697
00:54:05,390 --> 00:54:08,228
That only works because of the event log idea.

698
00:54:08,718 --> 00:54:12,900
The full history is kept and the prompt is built from whichever branch you're on.

699
00:54:14,222 --> 00:54:16,740
And look at the context management list.

700
00:54:17,390 --> 00:54:19,684
It's three of the patterns from today.

701
00:54:20,238 --> 00:54:24,100
First, an event log that gets converted into the model's input.

702
00:54:24,430 --> 00:54:27,268
Second, progressive disclosure through skill files.

703
00:54:27,662 --> 00:54:28,944
Third, compaction.

704
00:54:29,486 --> 00:54:40,260
The picture at the bottom shows what the model actually sees after compaction, the system prompt, then a summary of everything older, then the recent messages kept word for word.

705
00:54:40,654 --> 00:54:45,540
By default, Pi keeps about the last 20,000 tokens in full and summarizes the rest.

706
00:54:45,998 --> 00:54:50,980
And the summary has fixed headings like goal, progress, key decisions, and next steps.

707
00:54:51,470 --> 00:54:56,484
And the full original history stays in the session file, so nothing is actually lost.

708
00:54:57,582 --> 00:54:59,844
And here's the catch with being minimal.

709
00:55:00,384 --> 00:55:07,716
Pi runs with the same permissions as whatever process started it, and it never pops up a box asking, Are you sure?

710
00:55:08,238 --> 00:55:12,036
So think of it as always running in auto mode, with no protection.

711
00:55:12,558 --> 00:55:14,557
Remember the code act warning.

712
00:55:15,118 --> 00:55:17,892
An agent with a shell can do anything you can do.

713
00:55:18,304 --> 00:55:21,028
Pi's answer is that safety is your job.

714
00:55:21,390 --> 00:55:27,044
Run it in a container or write an extension that asks for confirmation before risky commands.

715
00:55:27,374 --> 00:55:30,020
That's a reasonable choice for an expert tool.

716
00:55:30,638 --> 00:55:39,076
It also means that if you run it on your laptop with your real credentials, a prompt injection hidden in a web page could reach all of them.

717
00:55:39,504 --> 00:55:50,692
Prompt injection, if you remember from lecture one, is when text the agent reads, like a web page or a file, contains instructions, and the agent follows them as if you'd given them.

718
00:55:51,118 --> 00:55:55,748
A harness with no permission checks has nothing standing between that text and your shell.

719
00:55:56,930 --> 00:55:57,950
To recap, hi.

720
00:55:58,510 --> 00:56:00,064
It's minimal by design.

721
00:56:00,238 --> 00:56:01,860
The community extends it.

722
00:56:02,094 --> 00:56:05,956
Its default tools are Red, Write, Edit, and Bash.

723
00:56:06,286 --> 00:56:08,064
It's a React style agent.

724
00:56:08,238 --> 00:56:16,388
It manages context with an event log, progressive disclosure, and compaction, and it has no safeguards built in.

725
00:56:17,550 --> 00:56:18,564
Let's wrap up.

726
00:56:19,598 --> 00:56:22,212
Today we covered workflows versus agents.

727
00:56:22,702 --> 00:56:32,292
The five workflow patterns, patterns for reasoning and acting, patterns for managing context, and a real harness that puts several of them together.

728
00:56:32,686 --> 00:56:35,140
If you remember one thing, make it this.

729
00:56:35,694 --> 00:56:37,764
These patterns are the vocabulary.

730
00:56:38,126 --> 00:56:44,900
When you look at any agent system, including the one you're building for your project, ask, where does the control flow live?

731
00:56:45,134 --> 00:56:46,784
How does it reason and act?

732
00:56:46,864 --> 00:56:50,148
And what's in the context window at each step and why?

733
00:56:50,606 --> 00:56:53,412
Let's run our briefing through it one last time.

734
00:56:53,774 --> 00:56:59,904
The outer structure, the sections the city always wants, is a palm chain with gates, so it's predictable.

735
00:57:00,302 --> 00:57:05,924
Inside the cost section, an orchestrator sends one worker per busmaker, and those run in parallel.

736
00:57:06,350 --> 00:57:10,148
Each worker is a React agent that writes code to do its arithmetic.

737
00:57:10,606 --> 00:57:20,036
A checker with search tools verifies every number, Swittic style, and when a check fails, the worker gets a written reflection and tries again.

738
00:57:20,430 --> 00:57:25,892
And the whole time the context is managed with a log, notes, masking, and isolation.

739
00:57:26,350 --> 00:57:28,196
None of those pieces is new.

740
00:57:28,526 --> 00:57:32,356
The design work is choosing which ones you need and leaving out the rest.

741
00:57:33,518 --> 00:57:39,268
On Wednesday, we'll talk about memory, which is how agents remember things across steps and across sessions.

742
00:57:39,950 --> 00:57:40,784
See you then.

